   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 296 of the laws of 2017 relating to a certain\nagreement for a payment in lieu of taxes, in relation to payment in lieu of\ntaxes",
    "basePrintNo": "S8951",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8951-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1, Chap 296 of 2017",
    "lawSection": "Taxation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8951\n\nSPONSOR: FLANAGAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 296 of the laws of 2017 relating to a certain\nagreement for a payment in lieu of taxes, in relation to payment in lieu\nof taxes\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill corrects a technical issue with legislation Chaptered in 2017,\nwherein the holding company was determined to be making \"payments in\nlieu of taxes\" as opposed to making \"payment of taxes\".\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends references to payment of taxes, to refer instead to\npayments in lieu of taxes.\n\nSection 2. This act shall take effect on the same date and in the same\nmanner as the 2017 Chapter.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nQualified Empire Zone Enterprises (QEZE) are vital businesses which\nencourage job growth and foster economic development. During the rele-\nvant years, the Qualified Company paid rent to a limited liability\ncompany, formed for the sole purpose of acquiring and holding this prop-\nerty, through a qualified exchange, which is an affiliate of the Quali-\nfied Company by common ownership and control.  Due to a technicality\ninvolving these commonly owned and controlled companies, the Department\nof Taxation and Finance would not honor the QEZE tax credit. As a\nresult, the Qualified Company, which had been approved for a tax credit\nand had been audited by the state during the relevant time period, was\nlater denied the credit. Furthermore, Tax and Finance has attempted to\nclaw back the credit from prior years.\n\nIn 2017, the Legislature enacted Chapter 296 of the Laws of 2017, which\nattempted to remedy the injustice that resulted from the unfavorable\ndetermination. However, because this legislation referred to payments\nmade as \"payment of taxes\", instead of \"payments in lieu of taxes\", the\nDepartment has opined that a technical correct is necessary. This legis-\nlation will carry out the original intent of the legislature.\n\nThe business has complied with the law and has relied on this tax credit\nfor operating expenses. This bill would allow the property owner to\nreceive their tax credit for the years 2012, 2013, 2014 and 2015, as was\nintended by the 2017 legislation.\n\nIn 2014, two similar pieces of legislation were signed into law. In\n2016, another similar piece of legislation was signed into law\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as\nChapter 296 of the Laws of 2017.\n",
